WEBVTT

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.760
Define the service before designing the cluster.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.426
Identify who will use it, which applications will call it, and when demand occurs.

00:00:06.426 --> 00:00:09.960
Ten analysts using interactive chat create a different load from an overnight

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.520
document-processing pipeline.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.520
Inventory model types, quantizations, context lengths, embedding workloads,

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.685
and any vision or multimodal processing.

00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:22.555
Each combination has distinct VRAM, system memory, storage, and acceleration requirements.

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.035
Separate latency from throughput.

00:00:24.035 --> 00:00:28.480
Latency is the time a user waits for useful output, including queueing, prompt processing,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:29.190
and generation.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.575
Throughput is the amount of work completed over time.

00:00:31.575 --> 00:00:35.640
Batching may improve total throughput while increasing the wait for an individual request.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.375
Define targets such as acceptable time to first token, requests per minute,

00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:41.700
concurrent sessions, and maximum queue depth.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.440
Use representative prompts because short synthetic tests can hide the cost of long

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.370
contexts.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.120
Document privacy, uptime, and recovery expectations.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.910
Determine where prompts, retrieved documents, model outputs,

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.755
and logs may be stored or transmitted.

00:00:53.755 --> 00:00:56.325
Decide whether planned maintenance is acceptable,

00:00:56.325 --> 00:00:58.800
whether a failed node must be bypassed automatically,

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.990
and how long service restoration may take.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.830
Convert budget into more than purchase price: include network equipment, storage,

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:09.235
support time, software, power, cooling, rack space, and expected utilization.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.160
Finally, challenge the cluster assumption.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.380
A larger single server, scheduled access to one workstation,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.240
or a managed service may satisfy the requirement with less operational burden.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.750
Build a cluster when measurements show a need for independent model roles,

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.410
aggregate concurrency, fault isolation, or horizontal growth.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.930
Requirements protect the project from solving an imagined scale problem while overlooking

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.160
the cost of operating a real distributed system.
